This examines the effect of solvent polarization on an FEP calculation
for Me4N+ --> Me4C in liquid benzene.

Addition of polarization is controlled on the last line of the par files.
POLSCX = POLSCS = 0.0 for no polarization (OPLS-AA).

POLSCX = 1.0 for addition of solute-solute polarization, i.e.,
inducible dipoles are added to solute atoms as specified below.
The electric field is computed at a solute atom including the
contributions from all atoms in other solutes.

POLSCS = 1.0 for addition of solvent by solute polarization.
Inducible dipoles are added to all solvent atoms as specified
below. Only "custom" solvents are allowed to have inducible
dipoles; a "custom" solvent requires a solvent Z-matrix.
The electric field is computed at a solvent atom including 
the contributions from all atoms in solutes. A 10-Angstrom
cutoff is applied. Specifically, for a given solvent molecule,
all distances specified by the solute-solvent cutoff procedure
are computed. If any is less than 10-A, all inducible dipoles
are included for that solvent molecule. E.g., for ICUT = 5,
all distances between non-H atoms of the solutes and the
solvent atoms specified in the ICUTAS array are computed; if
any is less than 10 A, the inducible dipoles are included for
that solvent molecule.

Solvent-solvent polarization is not allowed as it is implicit
in the parameterization of the OPLS-AA solvent models.
Solute by solvent polarization is also not treated as it
is normally not substantial. The key problems that are addressed
by the present polarization treatment are ions in solution and
polar molecules in low-dielectric solvents.

Currently, atoms that are assigned polarizabilites have the following
types PSYM; the corresponding polarizabilites are in PVAL. The PVALs
are scaled by POLSCX and POLSCS, and the utilized values are listed in
the output ot files.
